```rust
fn take_ownership(s: String) {
    println!("got: {}", s);
}   // s is dropped (freed) here, at end of scope

fn main() {
    let s = String::from("hello");
    take_ownership(s);          // ownership moves into the function
    println!("{}", s);          // error[E0382]: use of moved value: `s`
}
```

The compiler's actual message names the exact problem: `s` was moved into `take_ownership`, which drops it when the function returns — so by the time `main`'s `println!` runs, `s` doesn't own anything anymore. This is precisely the shape of bug [stack-vs-heap-allocation](stack-vs-heap-allocation) and [manual-memory-management](manual-memory-management) covered as a runtime crash (use-after-free) — Rust catches the identical mistake before the program ever runs.

You don't have to move ownership just to look at or temporarily use a value — you can **borrow** a reference to it instead. The borrow checker enforces one rule at compile time: for any value, you can have **either** any number of immutable borrows (`&T`), **or** exactly one mutable borrow (`&mut T`) — never both at once.

```rust
let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];
let r1 = &v;
let r2 = &v;              // fine: multiple immutable borrows
println!("{} {}", r1[0], r2[0]);

let r3 = &mut v;           // error if r1/r2 are still in use here
```

The rule isn't arbitrary pedantry — it's exactly the condition that makes a data race on this value structurally impossible: nobody can be reading through `r1`/`r2` while someone else mutates through `r3`, because the compiler won't let both borrows coexist in the first place.
